  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "2a6dd8ae-043c-4ee8-9de4-d00aab69bbab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Think about your last project. What changed between your first version and your final version?*\n- *Why do you think those changes happened?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on concrete changes they made between versions (e.g., adding features, fixing bugs, adjusting scope, responding to testing or feedback). Listen for patterns around planning, testing, revising, and changing scope. Use those responses to surface that this capstone is built around the same process, but applied across one longer, continuous project instead of restarting each unit.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a6dd8ae-043c-4ee8-9de4-d00aab69bbab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5952818-e08e-4631-9a89-f1febd8c6f11",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into teams of three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each team of students a copy of the Capstone Project Handout.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through these project goals. Emphasize that these describe what they will practice and demonstrate throughout the capstone, not a checklist of features they must build. Reinforce that the goals will be revisited across milestones as their project evolves.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the first page of the Project Handout. Let them know this page explains how the capstone is structured (benchmarks, milestones, and planning workflow), not the details of what they need to build yet.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What do you notice about the structure of this capstone project?*\n- *What do you wonder?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate how this project is structured differently from previous unit projects. Listen for students noticing ideas like milestones, benchmarks, iteration, planning over time, and not “finishing everything at once.” Use their responses to reinforce that this capstone supports ongoing revision and decision-making across one larger project. \n\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "During this discussion, explicitly tell students how their projects will be shared at the end of the capstone (for example: in-class gallery walk, school audience, or online). Emphasize that they will have time to prepare and choose what version to present, and that sharing expectations may influence how they scope and refine their work.\n\n\nIf questions about the Project Leader don't surface naturally, point it out to students and let them know that you'll talk about what that means shortly.\n\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5952818-e08e-4631-9a89-f1febd8c6f11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df729103-3a0a-4562-a607-10a70725daa0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df729103-3a0a-4562-a607-10a70725daa0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b7c81b7e-9250-401f-8a28-e8f3fdad86ab",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define the terms benchmark and milestone for students. Connect to previous unit projects where they've used milestones before — this capstone uses the same structure, but across a longer arc. Emphasize that benchmarks and milestones work together: milestones describe what to show, benchmarks are when you pause to show it.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain when each benchmark will happen and which milestones students will reflect on at each point. Emphasize that benchmarks are check-in moments, not deadlines, and that students can and should revisit and revise their idea, scope, or system flow at any time. At each benchmark, students will also complete a short on-platform reflection. Frame benchmarks as a way to pause, reflect, and stay oriented—not as a signal to stop iterating.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the rubric on pages 2 and 3 of the Project Handout. Emphasize that the rubric focuses on decision-making, clarity, and process alongside technical work, not a checklist of features. Let students know they will revisit this rubric throughout the capstone as they make and explain design choices.\n\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify what the rubric values and how success is defined beyond specific features. Listen for students noticing themes like clarity of purpose, intentional scope, explanation of decisions, iteration over time, and usability—not just “what to build.” Use this discussion to reinforce that success is about alignment and reasoning, not checking off features.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students do not bring up scope during the rubric discussion, use the next slide to explicitly define it. This term appears in the rubric and will be referenced throughout the project, so students need a shared understanding early on."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b7c81b7e-9250-401f-8a28-e8f3fdad86ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc289593-7605-45d4-b2d3-cb962a1de3b8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc289593-7605-45d4-b2d3-cb962a1de3b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85535ac8-5d82-4e6a-b5ee-2c844f018b11",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what scope means in the context of this project. Give a concrete example to make it tangible — for instance, a version that lets users search and filter results but does not yet include saving or sharing. Emphasize that scope is about making intentional choices for \"this version\", not limiting what the project could become later.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a copy of the Kanban Project Planning Board, a Project Leader Guide, a manila folder, a stack of sticky notes, and tape or glue. Let students know this planning board will be used throughout the capstone to track work, reflect at benchmarks, and support iteration—not just today.\n\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to tape or glue the Kanban Project Planning Board to one side of their manila folder. This folder will be used throughout the capstone to organize planning artifacts and reflections.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to tape or glue the Project Leader Guide to the other side of their manila folder. Take a moment to go over the Project Leader role. Ensure students understand that the Project Leader helps the team stay organized and on track — they are not a boss, but a facilitator. This role rotates at each benchmark, so every team member will take a turn. Answer any questions before moving on. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85535ac8-5d82-4e6a-b5ee-2c844f018b11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9856afe5-c7f1-4ebc-8625-ffb421c5736c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Software teams often use a backlog to keep track of the tasks they plan to work on and the order they’ll tackle them. For this capstone, we’ll use a similar idea to break milestones into manageable tasks and track progress using the Kanban Project Planning Board. Today, you’ll start identifying the tasks your group needs to focus on first.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9856afe5-c7f1-4ebc-8625-ffb421c5736c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5499da70-adb7-4aae-8ed0-f655c2e49b96",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that teams will break each milestone into a small set of tasks that are in scope for this version of their project. Students should write one task per sticky note and place them on the other side of their folder so the Project Leader can move tasks onto the Kanban board as work begins.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students review the goal for Milestone 1 in their Project Handout and discuss the tasks they think they will need to complete to reach that goal. Once groups agree on the tasks, have students write each task on a sticky note and place it in the TO DO column of their Kanban Board.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a moment to confirm who the first Project Leader will be. Remind them that this role includes maintaining the Kanban Board by moving tasks into In Progress and Done, which requires checking in with teammates over the next few days. Let students know the Project Leader role will rotate at each benchmark.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt groups to decide which Milestone 1 tasks they want to start with first. Remind students that they can add new tasks if needed or remove tasks that are no longer relevant. Emphasize that they should stay focused on Milestone 1 before identifying tasks for future milestones.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that Milestone 1 focuses on clarifying intent and scope, not writing code. If groups begin jumping into implementation, redirect them to explaining who their app is for, what problem it solves, and what this first version will include.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5499da70-adb7-4aae-8ed0-f655c2e49b96",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b35cb09-7845-404f-96ba-996d96b6541b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b35cb09-7845-404f-96ba-996d96b6541b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "194dbb82-dba9-46cb-a62d-c5acf59b1d4b",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 1 to begin Milestone 1: Intent and Scope. Remind them that they can choose whichever tool best supports their current thinking and move between tools as their ideas evolve.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  As students work in the Milestone 1 level choices, ask guiding questions such as:\n* Who is your app for?\n* What problem are you trying to solve?\n* What are you intentionally including in this first version?\n* What are you leaving out for now?\n\nUse these check-ins to confirm students are moving from ideas toward clear intent and scope, rather than jumping straight into code.\n",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1: Intent and Scope",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to experiment with both tools in the Milestone 1 Hub. Clarifying and refining their ideas now will save time when they begin building. Set clear pacing expectations: Milestone 1 can typically be completed by the end of today's class or early next class. Prioritize clarity of intent and scope over polish or completeness.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "194dbb82-dba9-46cb-a62d-c5acf59b1d4b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "956d95fd-aa7c-469a-bb92-0be558e30487",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "956d95fd-aa7c-469a-bb92-0be558e30487",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39c927fc-8b24-45d6-94b1-4a198677f6b8",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to review the Milestone 1 goal in their Capstone Project Handout and assess where their team is. Have them update their Kanban Board — moving tasks to In Progress or Done as appropriate. Let them know their thinking around idea and scope may continue to evolve across the next lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students wrap up, ensure they are discussing and aligning as a team. Listen for shared understanding around audience, problem, and scope.\n\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few volunteers to share or facilitate a quick pair-and-share. Focus on how ideas or scope shifted today — not on polishing responses.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39c927fc-8b24-45d6-94b1-4a198677f6b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "920dc160-b813-48ab-b02a-8a62401bd2f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "afb1b101-b03b-471a-b029-3a2747e58e2b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\n- *Are these ready to be a task cards?*\n- *If not, how would you rewrite them?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that effective task cards are specific, actionable, and scoped, not vague or overly broad. Listen for students suggesting ways to rewrite tasks so they describe a clear action that can be started and completed. Use this discussion to reinforce that good task cards support planning and iteration, not perfection.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "afb1b101-b03b-471a-b029-3a2747e58e2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3788051d-41ba-47aa-bd1e-b4d2e121e6e8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "629143ce-939f-4ab4-8f95-cdd300070b9e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Frame today as a transition, not a handoff. Explain that students should continue refining their Milestone 1 responses as needed while beginning work on Milestone 2. Before starting Milestone 2 work, have groups identify and post task cards on their Kanban Board for what they plan to work on today. Remind students that revisiting Milestone 1 (ideas, audience, scope) is expected as their system design becomes clearer.\n\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 1 if they still need to refine any Milestone 1 work, and to Level 2 to begin Milestone 2: System Explanation. Explain that the focus of Milestone 2 is describing how the app works — including user actions, app decisions, and what users see — not building or coding yet. Reinforce that students can move back and forth between Milestones 1 and 2 as their understanding evolves.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they are focusing on system flow, not UI polish or code. Ask guiding questions such as:\n- What does the user do first?\n- What does the app check or decide next?\n- What does the user see as a result?\n- Where might the app show different outcomes (e.g., empty, error, or success states)?\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2: System Explanation",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may try to jump ahead to designing screens or writing code. If this happens, redirect them to explaining the flow of one user interaction (user action → app decision → user sees) before worrying about layout or features.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "629143ce-939f-4ab4-8f95-cdd300070b9e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3788051d-41ba-47aa-bd1e-b4d2e121e6e8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5471ed6d-2f96-494c-bbbf-53f6bd05683d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5471ed6d-2f96-494c-bbbf-53f6bd05683d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3788051d-41ba-47aa-bd1e-b4d2e121e6e8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf208f3d-84b9-4a68-a609-ae02a7e56096",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to update their Kanban Board — moving tasks to In Progress or Done as appropriate. If students used the Sketch Lab, remind them to save their sketches to their Backpack. They'll use those sketches when they begin building in Web Lab. Remind students that if their ideas, audience, or scope shifted today, that's expected — they can continue refining in the next lesson.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students wrap up, listen for explanations that clearly describe one or more user interactions from start to finish (user action → app decision → user sees). Encourage students to notice where their explanation is still vague or skips steps, and to revise for clarity.\n\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few students to share one system interaction they explained clearly — or struggled to explain. Emphasize that being able to describe how an app works, even before it's built, is a key design and problem-solving skill.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf208f3d-84b9-4a68-a609-ae02a7e56096",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3788051d-41ba-47aa-bd1e-b4d2e121e6e8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45d0a4da-10cb-477e-b3a5-9f82c29a15e0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Look at one card your team is working on. Could one person finish this in one class period? If not, how could it be split?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students evaluate whether their task cards are appropriately scoped for one work session. Listen for students identifying tasks that are too large or vague and suggesting ways to break them into smaller, more actionable steps. Use this discussion to reinforce that well-scoped tasks support steady progress and clearer collaboration.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45d0a4da-10cb-477e-b3a5-9f82c29a15e0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98a57b03-0a03-46c3-8f61-42d84b1c9a3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55bf1f94-dc0b-4b16-a144-a9f6683d5e03",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Frame today as a continuation, not a reset. Students should keep refining their Milestone 2 system explanations — or revisit Milestone 1 if needed — before pausing for Benchmark A. Direct students to review the goals for Milestones 1 and 2 in their Capstone Project Handout to decide what to prioritize during work time.\n\nLet students know that Benchmark A will happen today. At that point, teams will pause to update their Kanban Board, rotate the Project Leader, and complete the Benchmark A Reflection on-platform (Level 3). Students who finish early may begin identifying task cards for Milestone 3.\n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may feel pressure to \"build everything\" once they reach Milestone 3. Encourage them to focus on getting one interaction working end-to-end (user action → app behavior → visible result) before expanding. Remind them that testing and revision are expected parts of the process."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55bf1f94-dc0b-4b16-a144-a9f6683d5e03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98a57b03-0a03-46c3-8f61-42d84b1c9a3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b19337d5-c3df-44dc-822b-84bf004ccd02",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b19337d5-c3df-44dc-822b-84bf004ccd02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98a57b03-0a03-46c3-8f61-42d84b1c9a3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3c3994e-5780-40ff-8988-f7a136a6a35e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 2 to refine their Milestone 2 work. Remind them that the focus is describing how the app works — user actions, app decisions, and what users see — not building or coding yet. Students can move between Milestones 1 and 2 as their understanding evolves. When a team has a clear system flow, direct them to identify task cards for Milestone 3.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that their system explanation could be understood by someone not in their group. Ask questions such as:\n- If I hadn’t seen your project before, could I follow what happens here?\n- Where might someone get confused about what the app does next?\n- Which part of this flow are you least confident about explaining?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some groups will feel \"ready\" to start Milestone 3 even if their system explanation isn't perfect. That's okay. Encourage them to move forward only once they can clearly explain one interaction, knowing they may return to refine their system explanation after testing.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3c3994e-5780-40ff-8988-f7a136a6a35e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98a57b03-0a03-46c3-8f61-42d84b1c9a3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f461c28-f81e-405a-90f1-b8217761344a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Signal to the class that it's time to pause for Benchmark A. Direct groups to stop their work and review their progress toward Milestones 1 and 2 using their Capstone Project Handout. Have groups update their Kanban Board — moving tasks to In Progress or Done — and rotate the Project Leader before moving on. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 3 — Benchmark A Reflection to complete their on-platform reflection. Remind them that this reflection asks them to explain their app's intent, scope, and system — and that their answers should reflect the thinking and decisions they've made so far, not a perfect final plan.\n",
        "progression_name": "Benchmark A Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Not all groups will be in the same place. Some may still be refining Milestone 2 while others are ready to move toward Milestone 3. That's expected. The reflection asks students to document where they are now — encourage them to be honest about what's clear and what's still evolving.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f461c28-f81e-405a-90f1-b8217761344a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98a57b03-0a03-46c3-8f61-42d84b1c9a3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eeeda521-ca9c-483d-bc05-2ac84a79bd31",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eeeda521-ca9c-483d-bc05-2ac84a79bd31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98a57b03-0a03-46c3-8f61-42d84b1c9a3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3e4dd21e-e546-4628-8737-4c18e343512d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Check that all groups have completed or are close to completing their Benchmark A Reflection in Level 3. Remind students to ensure their Kanban Board reflects their current progress before the next class.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some groups may want to rush through the benchmark to keep building. Slow this moment down. Emphasize that strong teams use checkpoints to adjust scope, refine plans, and redistribute work — this pause is what helps projects improve over time, not a break from progress.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3e4dd21e-e546-4628-8737-4c18e343512d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98a57b03-0a03-46c3-8f61-42d84b1c9a3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f217fb9-995a-40b3-aa75-aedbdcbf3c64",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Pick two tasks in your backlog. Which one should come first—and why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice prioritizing tasks based on dependencies, impact, and readiness. Listen for students explaining why one task needs to happen before another (e.g., it unblocks other work, tests a core interaction, or reduces uncertainty). Use this discussion to reinforce that task order is a decision, not a guess.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f217fb9-995a-40b3-aa75-aedbdcbf3c64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737830cf-1626-441a-abd2-59bd2056df45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cbd10a3-993d-408a-909b-75fa02a87f76",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Frame today as focused build time with intentional planning. Explain that students should continue working on Milestone 3 while using their Kanban Board to prioritize which tasks to tackle first. For teams that started Milestone 3 last class, have them review and adjust their task cards based on what they learned from testing before they starting today. Reinforce that revisiting earlier milestones is expected if testing reveals changes to scope or system behavior.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to focus on Milestone 3 work using their Kanban task cards. Explain that Level 2 tools are still available to help clarify or revise system flow if testing reveals confusion or gaps. Emphasize that today's goal is learning from testing, not finishing features.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for whether testing is informing their next steps. Ask questions such as:\n- What did you learn from testing this interaction?\n- Did anything behave differently than you expected?\n- Which task should change based on what you just observed?\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3: Build and Test",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This is the first time students open capstone.md. Let them know it's a running document they'll add to across the next several levels — they don't need to complete it all now. The goal today is filling in what they already know from Milestones 1 and 2 before they start building.\n\nSome students may treat testing as a quick check rather than a learning opportunity. Encourage them to slow down and observe behavior carefully — unexpected results often point to the most important improvements.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cbd10a3-993d-408a-909b-75fa02a87f76",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737830cf-1626-441a-abd2-59bd2056df45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "781455d9-0bc4-4475-95b9-c32235cb04ea",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "781455d9-0bc4-4475-95b9-c32235cb04ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737830cf-1626-441a-abd2-59bd2056df45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52de3cdd-ea64-4c3d-bfe3-d70bf7cad8af",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to update capstone.md based on what they worked on today. If teams revisited Milestone 1 or 2 thinking, have them update the relevant sections — app idea, audience, scope, or system — so capstone.md reflects how their thinking evolved through testing and discussion.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that strong documentation captures learning over time. Updating capstone.md after testing or discussion is exactly how real developers document changing decisions and make smarter choices in future iterations.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52de3cdd-ea64-4c3d-bfe3-d70bf7cad8af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "737830cf-1626-441a-abd2-59bd2056df45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47bee273-bea7-4a45-a8ef-2935c620c3f7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Name one thing your team did well last class that helped you work together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on effective collaboration strategies from the previous class. Listen for examples such as clear task division, communication, checking in with teammates, or using the Kanban Board to stay organized. Use these responses to reinforce that strong teamwork is an intentional skill that directly supports progress on complex projects.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47bee273-bea7-4a45-a8ef-2935c620c3f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "296b3c6f-ebb6-4eb6-9f48-3def43f1d6bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e25a0f6-c196-4ae9-8907-cc0ecfaccc00",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review today's focus with students. Explain that Day 5 continues Milestone 3 work, with teams using testing results to decide which task cards to move forward, split, or revise. Let students know that Benchmark B will happen today — teams will pause to update their Kanban Board, rotate the Project Leader, and complete the Benchmark B Reflection on-platform (Level 5). Remind students that revisiting earlier milestones is expected if building reveals changes to ideas, scope, or system behavior.\n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some teams may feel pressure to add many features quickly. Encourage students to slow down and focus on making a small number of interactions reliable and understandable. Strong projects grow from stable, well-tested pieces—not rushed additions.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e25a0f6-c196-4ae9-8907-cc0ecfaccc00",
        "lesson_activity.key": "296b3c6f-ebb6-4eb6-9f48-3def43f1d6bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "900daa9b-ff47-4d0c-a297-7ab0f78a97fe",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "900daa9b-ff47-4d0c-a297-7ab0f78a97fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "296b3c6f-ebb6-4eb6-9f48-3def43f1d6bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "11f79fa0-7fbd-476b-a894-595ba04b42fa",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to focus primarily on Milestone 3 work today. Explain that Level 1 and 2 tools are still available if they need to revisit or clarify their ideas or system behavior — especially when testing reveals unexpected results. Reinforce that students should let testing guide what they work on next.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, listen for whether testing is shaping their decisions. Ask questions such as:\n- What did you learn from testing this feature?\n- Did anything behave differently than expected?\n- What task should you tackle next based on what you observed?\n",
        "progression_name": "Benchmark B Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume revisiting Level 1 or 2 means they \"did something wrong.\" Reframe this as a strength — using earlier planning tools to make sense of new testing results is exactly how real developers improve systems over time.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "11f79fa0-7fbd-476b-a894-595ba04b42fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "296b3c6f-ebb6-4eb6-9f48-3def43f1d6bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "38947e90-2912-4d96-8942-94d3426f75d1",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "38947e90-2912-4d96-8942-94d3426f75d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "296b3c6f-ebb6-4eb6-9f48-3def43f1d6bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8da6b6cd-1c1e-4157-bc01-2c95e292566e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Check that all groups have completed or are close to completing their Benchmark B Reflection in Level 5. Remind students to ensure their Kanban Board is current and that their Testing Log in capstone.md reflects what they learned today.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some teams may feel pressure to \"finish\" Milestone 3 during this benchmark. Reframe this moment as a check on how well the app behaves, not how many features exist. Encourage teams to use testing results to decide which tasks are truly ready to move forward and which need to be refined, split, or postponed.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8da6b6cd-1c1e-4157-bc01-2c95e292566e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "296b3c6f-ebb6-4eb6-9f48-3def43f1d6bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02e16db5-cb15-4072-8ca3-61424d926eae",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Look at your team’s Kanban board. Discuss:\n- *One card that moved forward*\n- *One card that’s stuck*\n- *One card you think should be added or split*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how work is actually progressing, not just what is finished. Listen for students recognizing when tasks need to be split, rewritten, or reprioritized based on testing or new understanding. Use this discussion to reinforce that updating the Kanban board is part of the work, not a sign of falling behind.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02e16db5-cb15-4072-8ca3-61424d926eae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "805b3932-b7ae-4cd6-9789-1dc64a5fe67c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed109493-c525-4ded-b42e-fd9aed1de357",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Frame today as iterative work, not a reset. Explain that students should continue building and testing in Milestone 3 while revisiting Milestones 1 or 2 if testing reveals changes to their idea, scope, or system explanation. Before students begin working, have groups review and update their task cards on the Kanban Board so today’s work reflects their current priorities.\n\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If a team has a working interaction (user action → app behavior → visible result), encourage them to begin Milestone 4: Feedback & Iteration by getting feedback from peers, AI, or you. Emphasize that feedback may send them back to building and testing — that loop is expected.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed109493-c525-4ded-b42e-fd9aed1de357",
        "lesson_activity.key": "805b3932-b7ae-4cd6-9789-1dc64a5fe67c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f2fbcd6-b580-4263-a8af-2ad53204db51",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f2fbcd6-b580-4263-a8af-2ad53204db51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "805b3932-b7ae-4cd6-9789-1dc64a5fe67c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6835d4b-7163-4e72-a562-f083fda46698",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to focus primarily on Milestone 3: Build & Test today. Explain that Level 1 and 2 tools are still available if testing raises questions about scope, system flow, or user behavior. Reinforce that testing should guide what task cards they work on next, not the original plan alone.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, listen for whether testing is shaping their decisions. Ask questions such as:\n- What did testing tell you about this feature?\n- Did testing confirm your plan, or change it?\n- Which task card feels most urgent after seeing it run?\n- What’s one small adjustment you’re making based on what you observed?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As teams build, they may jump between tasks without finishing any of them. Encourage students to complete or pause tasks intentionally — either move a card to Done, or rewrite it before switching. This reinforces purposeful iteration instead of scattered progress.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6835d4b-7163-4e72-a562-f083fda46698",
        "lesson_activity.key": "805b3932-b7ae-4cd6-9789-1dc64a5fe67c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2d377010-239f-48c1-9097-30be503a3579",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2d377010-239f-48c1-9097-30be503a3579",
        "lesson_activity.key": "805b3932-b7ae-4cd6-9789-1dc64a5fe67c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b0035d0-a4f5-49dc-9275-c61b756158e3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to update capstone.md based on what they worked on today. If teams revisited Milestone 1 or 2, remind them to add to or revise earlier sections so capstone.md clearly shows how their thinking changed as a result of testing and discussion.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Watch for teams writing vague entries like \"we fixed bugs\" or \"we changed stuff.\" Prompt them to be specific: What changed? Why? What did testing reveal? Specific documentation helps teams decide what to build next — not just record what already happened.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b0035d0-a4f5-49dc-9275-c61b756158e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "805b3932-b7ae-4cd6-9789-1dc64a5fe67c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe41f2f5-99a8-4fd9-bcde-e1d92cd7de8e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *For your team today, choose one: on track, some concerns, blocked. What made you choose that today?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate their current project status using evidence from their work (e.g., task cards, testing results, or unclear decisions). Listen for students identifying specific blockers or uncertainties, not just general feelings. This helps surface where teams may need support, scope adjustment, or task re-prioritization today.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe41f2f5-99a8-4fd9-bcde-e1d92cd7de8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3a7877f-6e96-4522-9c86-4e74efbb2bbf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51ab9e83-38cc-4199-880a-99e6e88fda57",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Frame today as continued build time with intentional reflection. Explain that students should keep working on Milestone 3: Build & Test, using their Kanban Board to prioritize tasks based on what testing reveals. If a team has at least one working interaction (user action → app behavior → visible result), encourage them to begin Milestone 4: Feedback & Iteration by gathering feedback from peers, AI, or you. Reinforce that feedback may send them back to building or revising earlier milestones — that loop is expected. Remind students to update their task cards and capstone.md as their understanding evolves.\n\n\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Let students know that Benchmark C happens next class. Encourage teams to use today to get their app to a point they can explain and show — not finished, but demonstrable.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51ab9e83-38cc-4199-880a-99e6e88fda57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3a7877f-6e96-4522-9c86-4e74efbb2bbf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7579c99d-f518-4912-a61c-3756f4a1e473",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to focus on Milestone 3: Build & Test, with the expectation that testing will actively shape their next steps. Emphasize that today is about responding to what they observe, not just completing planned tasks. Remind students that returning to Level 1 or 2 is expected if testing raises questions about scope, assumptions, or system flow. Students who are ready can also move forward to Milestone 4 and start getting feedback from peers, AI, or the teacher.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, listen for how they’re using testing to make decisions. Ask questions like:\n- What did this test change about your plan?\n- What assumption did this feature challenge?\n- Which task became more important after seeing this run?\n- What’s clearer now than it was yesterday?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may interpret \"more features\" as progress. Redirect them toward clearer behavior instead. If a team is adding features quickly, ask them to explain what problem each new feature solves and what testing showed that justified it. This reinforces evidence-driven iteration, not just productivity.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7579c99d-f518-4912-a61c-3756f4a1e473",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3a7877f-6e96-4522-9c86-4e74efbb2bbf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4585764-4aaa-4695-b593-3466ab75633e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4585764-4aaa-4695-b593-3466ab75633e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3a7877f-6e96-4522-9c86-4e74efbb2bbf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6946142e-5896-4f49-8670-9b79ffd18dfc",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to update capstone.md based on what they actively worked on today. Emphasize that Day 7 entries should capture decisions made from testing, not just progress. If teams revisited Milestone 1 or 2, remind them to clearly explain what changed and why so capstone.md shows the evolution of their thinking.\n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use this wrap-up to shift teams from recording activity to justifying decisions. If responses sound procedural (\"we tested,\" \"we fixed bugs\"), prompt teams to connect actions to outcomes: \"What problem did this fix address?\" or \"What evidence told you this was the right change?\" This reinforces that capstone.md is a decision log, not a daily checklist."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6946142e-5896-4f49-8670-9b79ffd18dfc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b3a7877f-6e96-4522-9c86-4e74efbb2bbf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd54dabb-6367-4326-a079-cbdddab6a3a0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one way a teammate helped you without doing the work for you?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify productive support behaviors such as asking clarifying questions, suggesting next steps, testing together, or giving feedback—rather than taking control or writing code for someone else. Listen for examples that show collaboration, communication, and shared problem-solving, which directly connect to expectations for Milestone 4 feedback.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd54dabb-6367-4326-a079-cbdddab6a3a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3392a669-5f5d-48d2-910a-0f00b8d3421a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a580ff98-7ae0-407c-bc13-fae4a47019ef",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Frame today as a shift from mostly building to building + learning from feedback. Let students know they do not need a \"finished app\" to begin Milestone 4 — one working interaction is enough. Encourage teams to use feedback from peers, AI, or you to decide what to improve next and to update their Kanban Board accordingly. Let students know that Benchmark C will happen today — teams will pause to update their Kanban Board, rotate the Project Leader, and complete the Benchmark C Reflection on-platform (Level 7).\n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may think feedback comes after building. Reinforce that feedback is a tool for deciding what to build next — not a final step. Strong teams use feedback to prioritize, simplify, or even remove features. That's good judgment, not failure.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a580ff98-7ae0-407c-bc13-fae4a47019ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3392a669-5f5d-48d2-910a-0f00b8d3421a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ff96d69-e51a-4559-bd15-f84b4b1e523d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ff96d69-e51a-4559-bd15-f84b4b1e523d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3392a669-5f5d-48d2-910a-0f00b8d3421a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c16c48e-e280-4fc1-8dc9-2fd543179372",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students that this TAG feedback protocol is a tool they’ve used before and can choose to use again during Milestone 4: Feedback & Iteration. Emphasize that TAG is optional and flexible — teams can use it with peers, with you, or even adapt it when reflecting on AI feedback. Clarify that feedback does not need to happen all at once or with a full class switch; teams should use TAG when they feel ready for input.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some teams may not feel “ready” for feedback yet. Normalize that feedback is most useful once one interaction works end-to-end. Encourage teams to use TAG when they want help deciding what to improve next — not as a judgment of how finished their app is."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c16c48e-e280-4fc1-8dc9-2fd543179372",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3392a669-5f5d-48d2-910a-0f00b8d3421a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82396233-0d99-440f-b4b9-e9424496c365",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82396233-0d99-440f-b4b9-e9424496c365",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3392a669-5f5d-48d2-910a-0f00b8d3421a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "901697f9-f9da-4901-8973-5dc9abdc477d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Milestone 4: Feedback & Iteration. Explain that today's focus is learning from feedback, not finishing or polishing. Remind students they only need one working interaction to get useful feedback, and that feedback may send them back to building, revising earlier milestones, or adjusting scope.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students gather feedback, listen for:\n- What patterns are they hearing across feedback?\n- Are they revising tasks, not just collecting comments?\n- Are they deciding what not to change — and why?\n\nPrompt with:\n- “What feedback surprised you?”\n- “What feedback changed your plan?”\n- “What’s the first revision you’ll make based on this?”\n",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4: Feedback and Iteration",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may treat feedback as a checklist to complete. Reframe it as information for decision-making — strong teams decide which feedback to act on, which to defer, and which to reject. This is a key professional skill and aligns directly with the rubric's focus on judgment and iteration.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "901697f9-f9da-4901-8973-5dc9abdc477d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3392a669-5f5d-48d2-910a-0f00b8d3421a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9de28462-e802-4501-8151-24118928a5d2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Signal to the class that it's time to pause for Benchmark C. Direct all groups to stop their work and review their progress toward Milestone 4 using their Capstone Project Handout. Have groups update their Kanban Board and rotate the Project Leader before moving on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 7 — Benchmark C Reflection to complete their on-platform reflection. Remind them that this reflection asks them to describe the feedback they received, what they chose to change, and what they decided not to change — and why. Their answers should reflect honest decision-making, not a polished final product.\n",
        "progression_name": "Benchmark C Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Not all groups will be in the same place. Some may still be gathering feedback while others are ready to begin final polish. That's expected — the reflection asks students to document the decisions they've made so far, including what they're still working through.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9de28462-e802-4501-8151-24118928a5d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3392a669-5f5d-48d2-910a-0f00b8d3421a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a05061db-e860-4f46-b26b-183dff65acd2",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a05061db-e860-4f46-b26b-183dff65acd2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3392a669-5f5d-48d2-910a-0f00b8d3421a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e293c7ea-9bb4-4ff8-9ac9-70d7216f2c70",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Check that all groups have completed or are close to completing their Benchmark C Reflection in Level 7. Remind students to ensure their Kanban Board is current and that their Feedback Log in capstone.md reflects the decisions they made today.\n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Teams may focus on what they changed instead of why they changed it. Push students to connect revisions back to feedback sources — peer comments, AI suggestions, testing results. This reinforces that Milestone 4 is about judgment and decision-making, not just iteration for iteration's sake."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e293c7ea-9bb4-4ff8-9ac9-70d7216f2c70",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3392a669-5f5d-48d2-910a-0f00b8d3421a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0e7a91d-48c8-4345-b2b4-e45e96d01e8d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If your team had 10 extra minutes last class, what would you have used them for?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify unfinished, high-value work and begin prioritizing next steps. Listen for responses that reference testing results, feedback, or clarity issues (not just “adding features”). Use this to reinforce that effective teams decide what matters most next based on evidence, time, and impact—not just what’s left on the backlog.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0e7a91d-48c8-4345-b2b4-e45e96d01e8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "588ea4d1-84d3-455a-80b1-317da9913888"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9cd46315-e6b2-4393-915b-45240c16b82a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Frame today as final decision-making, not feature expansion. Explain that students should focus on preparing a version of their app they are ready to stand behind and explain. Teams may still revisit any milestone (1–4) if testing or feedback reveals gaps, but the goal is to stabilize, clarify, and justify their choices rather than add new functionality.\n\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 8, to complete levels 8-10. Explain that today is about communication and synthesis, not adding new features. Students should decide what version of their app they are presenting and ensure it behaves reliably, reflects feedback they chose to act on, and aligns with their intended audience. Remind students to complete all sections of capstone.md — their Final Reflection will draw on what they documented there.\n- **Level 8:** Students polish and finalize their app.\n- **Level 9:** Students use AI Chat to get help organizng their presentation and how they'll explain their project.\n- **Level 10:** Students complete their Final Reflection. \n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for signs that they are making intentional tradeoffs. Ask questions such as:\n- Which interaction feels most solid right now?\n- What feedback did you choose not to act on — and why?\n- What would you explain first if someone saw your app for 30 seconds?\n- What would you want working reliably if you had to stop today?\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 5: Final App and Presentation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9cd46315-e6b2-4393-915b-45240c16b82a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "588ea4d1-84d3-455a-80b1-317da9913888"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0fb086cc-55d3-45f4-96c1-7b26a709702a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Final Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may feel pressure to \"make it perfect.\" Reinforce that strong capstone projects show intentional choices, not maximum features. A clear, stable app with a well-explained rationale is more compelling than an overbuilt one."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0fb086cc-55d3-45f4-96c1-7b26a709702a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "588ea4d1-84d3-455a-80b1-317da9913888"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa9648b0-3566-40fe-a99b-2fba797d5dac",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa9648b0-3566-40fe-a99b-2fba797d5dac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "588ea4d1-84d3-455a-80b1-317da9913888"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b0a6e40-b41f-4880-88bf-0c206c0091f6",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Check that all students have completed their Final Reflection in Level 10 and that capstone.md is fully filled in. Let students know that next class is presentation day — they should come ready to share and explain their app and the decisions behind it.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Listen for whether teams can explain why they kept, refined, or postponed features. If reflections sound like status updates, prompt deeper thinking: \"Which part of your app are you most confident in right now?\" or \"What did you decide not to improve yet — and why?\" Strong projects show judgment and prioritization, not just effort."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b0a6e40-b41f-4880-88bf-0c206c0091f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "588ea4d1-84d3-455a-80b1-317da9913888"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bd049bf-02e8-4fbe-a545-3ec9c8ee3c70",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What part of your process improved over these two weeks, even if the project isn’t perfect?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how their way of working improved over the project (planning, breaking down tasks, testing, revising, using feedback, or collaborating), rather than focusing on whether the app is finished. Listen for students naming concrete process shifts and reinforce that growth in process is a key outcome of the capstone.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bd049bf-02e8-4fbe-a545-3ec9c8ee3c70",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97abe09a-3a08-4f5a-8c84-5d86e43572e6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today isn’t about changing your app one more time. It’s about explaining what this version represents — what you focused on, what you tested, what you changed, and what you chose to leave out.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97abe09a-3a08-4f5a-8c84-5d86e43572e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21a97d1b-ce8d-4834-94ae-ab9c88ae2c83",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Frame today as a showcase and explanation day. Have students prepare to demonstrate their current app version and explain the key decisions behind it — what they focused on, what they tested, what feedback influenced their choices, and what they intentionally left out. Emphasize that students are not expected to add new features today; the goal is to clearly communicate how their app works and why it looks the way it does.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may feel pressure to “lock everything in” today. Reinforce that strong capstone work shows intentional choices, not maximum features. Encourage teams to prioritize clarity, reliability, and explanation over polish — a clear, well-justified app is more powerful than a rushed one."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21a97d1b-ce8d-4834-94ae-ab9c88ae2c83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adcfe8ba-d796-45b1-ace4-91aef90e1812",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "adcfe8ba-d796-45b1-ace4-91aef90e1812",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1935a65e-afa9-46b0-95a0-8ed081abe35d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to pages 2 and 3 of their Capstone Project Handout where the rubric is located. Have students read each category and identify where their project currently falls for this version. Emphasize that this is a snapshot — not a final judgment — meant to help them understand strengths, gaps, and next steps.\n\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students treating this as a checklist rather than a reflection. Prompt them to reference specific evidence from their app or capstone.md (e.g., “Where do you see this in your project?”). Pay close attention to the Responsible AI & Ethical Reflection row and ask students how their decisions about AI use reflect judgment and transparency.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Reinforce that self-assessment is a professional design habit, not just a score. Frame the rubric as a tool for explaining decisions: what they prioritized, what they tested, and what they intentionally left out. If students struggle, model a sentence starter like: “Right now, our project fits here because…”\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1935a65e-afa9-46b0-95a0-8ed081abe35d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a8fb6be6-a906-4e6b-8bd0-95f124f80ebd",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a8fb6be6-a906-4e6b-8bd0-95f124f80ebd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7972d60-7fc8-4686-b617-09636d0fce1d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Give students a short window for final intentional refinement — not a full build session. Emphasize that today is about intentional refinement, not adding every possible feature. Students should use what they learned from testing, peer feedback, and self-assessment to decide what small changes will most improve how their app behaves and how clearly they can explain it.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students making purposeful decisions rather than chasing completeness. Ask students to point to the rubric row or feedback that influenced their current priorities. Check that teams are documenting why they made changes in their Feedback Log in capstone.md, not just listing what they changed.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may feel pressure to treat this like a \"final polish day.\" Reframe it as a decision day. Encourage students to choose a small number of high-impact improvements they can clearly justify. Remind them that a capstone is strongest when students can explain why this version looks the way it does — not when it has the most features.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7972d60-7fc8-4686-b617-09636d0fce1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55ca7a65-3ebc-45ab-8d6b-a7822b320bfe",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55ca7a65-3ebc-45ab-8d6b-a7822b320bfe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "38d2c435-6e94-4a34-a120-c0e7f84bf587",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student several sticky notes for leaving feedback during the gallery walk.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This slide assumes an in-class gallery walk. If you’re using a different format for public sharing, revise this and the next slide as needed to match how students will view and respond to each other’s work. See the Capstone Teacher Guide for sharing options.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "38d2c435-6e94-4a34-a120-c0e7f84bf587",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8667b374-fa23-4d40-975b-164cf7b5ceb4",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8667b374-fa23-4d40-975b-164cf7b5ceb4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60f88e29-49ca-40f4-a6e3-9746aae963fd",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students display their current capstone app versions. Students move around the room to explore classmates’ work and leave feedback using sticky notes. Encourage them to focus on how the app works as a system — what the user does, what the app does in response, and what the user sees — rather than judging polish or completeness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students move through the gallery, listen for feedback that connects to system behavior and user experience. Prompt with questions like:\n- Is it clear what the user can do first?\n- What happens when filters or controls are used?\n- Are results, labels, and counts transparent and accurate?\n- Does the app behave the way the creator described?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This slide assumes an in-class gallery walk. If you’re using a different format for public sharing, revise this and the previous setup slide to match how students will view and respond to each other’s work.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60f88e29-49ca-40f4-a6e3-9746aae963fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e867c94-f2af-4198-ac02-f22868c75aeb",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e867c94-f2af-4198-ac02-f22868c75aeb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70ade1d5-266b-48c7-95dd-7c773359feeb",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You didn’t just build an app — you practiced making decisions over time. This final reflection is your chance to explain how your project evolved: what you tested, what changed, what stayed the same, and why. Focus on the thinking behind this version, not whether it feels “finished.”",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70ade1d5-266b-48c7-95dd-7c773359feeb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c88cb40d-548d-4ce3-a723-5dced8068ee5",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete their Final Reflection in Level 10 if they haven't already. Emphasize that this is about explaining their decisions and learning across milestones — not listing everything they built. Encourage students to reference specific moments where testing, feedback, or constraints shaped what they chose to improve, revise, or leave out.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write, prompt them to anchor reflections in concrete examples:\n- a test that revealed an unexpected behavior\n- feedback that changed a design or priority\n- a decision to simplify, delay, or remove a feature\n- a moment when AI helped — or didn’t — and why\n\nProbe with questions like:\n- “What evidence told you this was the right decision?”\n- “How did your system behave differently after this change?”\n- “What would you keep working on if you had more time?”\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The Benchmark Reflections and `capstone.md` provide assessable evidence for:\n- Project Planning & Intentional Design\n- Responsible AI & Ethical Reflection\n- Collaboration & Professional Practice\n\nThe working app provides assessable evidence for:\n- Functionality & Technical Implementation\n- User-Centered Design & Usability\n\nTeachers may use a combination of levels, capstone.md, the Kanban Board, and the showcased app to assess the full rubric.\n\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c88cb40d-548d-4ce3-a723-5dced8068ee5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "53240202-3449-4587-9643-68c5f3298196"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe353a34-a609-4bc3-97af-4969ef9443a1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
